{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":73506350,"ns":0,"title":"Cubicity","extract":"In the mathematical field of graph theory, cubicity is a graph invariant defined to be the smallest dimension such that a graph can be realized as the intersection graph of axis-parallel unit cubes in Euclidean space. Cubicity was introduced by Fred S. Roberts in 1969, along with a related invariant called boxicity that considers the smallest dimension needed to represent a graph as the intersection graph of axis-parallel rectangles in Euclidean space.","description":"Graph invariant defined from axis-parallel unit cubes","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}